context if we could have that picture.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.579
So, here we have two animals,

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:03.090
the human (us) and the coral.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:05.820
Maybe you could tell us a bit about

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:08.160
sleep in the animal kingdom. I mean,

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:14.849
we all have seen our dogs or cats sleep, but I mean how widespread is sleep among animals?

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:23.490
Yeah, well one approach to understanding the purpose of sleep in our brain and sleep in general is to look at how

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:30.329
different types of animals sleep. Do all animals sleep the same way? Are there some animals that don't sleep? And

00:06:30.669 --> 00:06:37.529
by studying animals ranging from simple organisms such as this brain coral to more complex

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:44.190
organisms, we may gain insight into the function of sleep, both in general and in our brain.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:52.859
So, does a coral sleep? Well, they retreat into their calcium house in the daytime,

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:58.380
so these corals might actually be sleeping while the awake, I presume awake, human is

00:06:59.169 --> 00:07:01.589
observing them. Right... and flies?

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:09.299
There's a lot of work studying fruit flies in the last 10 years, and they've been shown to clearly

00:07:09.849 --> 00:07:13.769
engage in sleep. They have many parallels with sleep in

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:18.419
human's sleep . . . in mammals. They

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:24.089
. . .their sleep becomes more fragmented when they get older, there're changes in the brain

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:32.399
chemistry and expression of genes that are very similar to those in humans. And this is proven to be a very powerful

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:35.199
organism in which to

00:07:35.199 --> 00:07:37.769
try to gain insight into the function of sleep,

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:45.840
because in the fruit fly, you can manipulate the genome, you can turn genes on, you can turn genes off, you can induce mutations,

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:51.150
and look at how that affects their sleep. And if you find some gene

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:54.000
that's very interesting that affects their sleep,

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.729
then you can look in mammals to see if it plays a similar role; and that can give us clues to the purpose of sleep.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:06.569
Now, you study a much closer relative to us than brain coral. You study birds.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:08.620
um

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:10.979
How did you get interested in studying birds?

00:08:10.979 --> 00:08:16.229
And why do you think they're important for helping us to understand sleep in general?

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.259
Yeah, I actually worked first with

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:24.900
humans and then went to study birds. And birds are an extremely

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:29.340
exciting group in which to study sleep because they're the only

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:34.950
taxonomic group that has evolved sleep patterns very much like ours and

00:08:35.589 --> 00:08:37.739
those observed in the rest of the mammals.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.390
Why don't you tell us about what our sleep . .  our basic sleep pattern is and then

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.029
talk about how birds are like that?

00:08:45.029 --> 00:08:50.759
So, in humans and other mammals, we engage in two general types of sleep and these are

00:08:52.029 --> 00:08:53.410
measured by

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:58.140
looking at changes in brain activity in the sleeping human. There is

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:04.079
REM sleep or rapid eye movement sleep. During this state, the brain actually is

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:09.420
exhibiting a pattern of activity that's very similar to that observed when we're awake.

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:12.659
But we're unresponsive. We're clearly asleep.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:16.349
Then, there's another state called non- REM sleep.

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:19.230
Which the brainwaves are

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:21.579
markedly different

00:09:21.579 --> 00:09:23.579
and

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.659
When you look at the brainwave activity of a bird that's sleeping,

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:29.460
they do the same thing.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.229
They exhibit both these states.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:37.859
They have REM sleep, their eyes dart around, their brain is very activated very much like when they're awake.

00:09:38.859 --> 00:09:44.879
They lose some muscle tone, just like we do. Maybe, if you have a cat,

00:09:46.029 --> 00:09:51.508
sometimes if they sleep on top of a television when they enter REM sleep, they lose muscle tone

00:09:51.509 --> 00:09:53.639
and they roll off the TV and fall on the floor.

00:09:54.369 --> 00:09:56.369
That's one way to see this at home.

00:09:58.239 --> 00:10:00.509
Try it out. Try it at home, yes.

00:10:01.899 --> 00:10:03.100
and

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:05.100
if you have

00:10:05.169 --> 00:10:12.599
Dogs will often move their paws and whimper as if chasing rabbits, and they're in REM sleep. But, so these two

00:10:13.179 --> 00:10:15.179
sleep states that we have

00:10:15.669 --> 00:10:21.089
birds have them also; and they don't have similar sleep states because they share a common

00:10:21.639 --> 00:10:25.889
ancestor that had the same sleep states. They seem to have evolved these patterns independently.

00:10:26.499 --> 00:10:29.999
So, the mammal could get the RAM non REM sleep,

00:10:29.999 --> 00:10:36.569
birds get it, too? Yeah, so the closest living relatives to birds are the crocodiles and other

00:10:36.939 --> 00:10:43.168
reptiles, and they don't show these two distinct states. They clearly sleep. Crocodiles probably don't dream, at least the way we do.

00:10:43.989 --> 00:10:45.989
I don't know. We'll find out.

00:10:47.769 --> 00:10:52.798
So and now you found one really amazing difference between

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:57.029
us and birds, which is I mean when we go to sleep,

00:10:57.759 --> 00:11:03.359
the lights are all out in the sense that our whole brain is involved in sleep; whereas with birds. it's not always the case, right?

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:09.720
Yeah, birds can do a very interesting thing and that is they can sleep with one eye open.

00:11:10.419 --> 00:11:12.419
This had been observed

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:14.619
decades ago

00:11:14.619 --> 00:11:16.259
But nobody had really addressed

00:11:16.259 --> 00:11:21.238
what was the function of sleeping with one eye open. Now, this might seem like an obvious question,

00:11:21.429 --> 00:11:28.319
and the obvious answer would be that they're watching for something dangerous while sleeping. You can imagine that sleeping is a

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:32.789
very dangerous state to engage in, especially if you live with predators;

00:11:33.579 --> 00:11:34.809
and

00:11:34.809 --> 00:11:37.649
so, we were interested in testing whether

00:11:38.289 --> 00:11:41.009
birds use this to watch for predators.

00:11:41.139 --> 00:11:48.029
And maybe I can just back up one second and talk about the image that's currently on the screen.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:54.989
For many animals, there really is this fundamental conflict between the need to be awake and

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:55.890
also

00:11:55.890 --> 00:12:03.840
the need to get their sleep. An interest in this conflict dates back thousands of years as shown in the legend depicted in this

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:05.890
piece of art.

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:07.150
According to this

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:15.119
legend, in order to stay safe while sleeping, one crane in a group would stay awake holding a stone and

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:19.500
If he started to fall asleep. He would relax and drop the stone and

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:22.889
thereby awaken everybody and

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:27.179
that's how they were thought to keep themselves safe. Now,

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:33.810
there's absolutely no scientific evidence that the cranes do this, but as I was discussing

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:40.709
birds can sleep with one eye open; and when they have one eye opened, the half of the brain connected to that eye is

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:43.330
kind of in a

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:45.330
wake or slightly drowsy state

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:49.170
and so we were interested in learning whether

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:58.139
they can actually choose when to sleep this way or when to sleep with both eyes closed because they don't sleep

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:02.460
only this way they spend a lot of time sleeping with both eyes closed; and

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:05.189
so, what we wanted to do was find a way to

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:10.859
make them feel a little nervous when they were sleeping. Just a little edgy. A little edgy.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:16.800
And so, we thought about various ways . . . you know, show them a cat or something.

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:19.720
But

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:23.160
And while we were trying to figure out how to test this,

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:30.450
I was observing some of our ducks that we were studying in the laboratory and I saw that two of them at night were

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:36.359
floating in the water next to each other; and while they were doing that, they were closing the eye

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:43.410
that faced each other and opening the eye that was facing out from each other. And that

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:47.460
right then and there the birds designed the experiment for us

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:52.350
People often say oh, that's such an elegant study, and I have to say well

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:54.990
It came from a bird brand

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:58.929
Did you acknowledge the Ducks in your paper? Oh?

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:02.720
So

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:06.130
So this is this is the experiment kind of the effort of the way yeah?

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:10.390
The upper image shows the actual experimental apparatus

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:14.710
and so what we did is we literally put our ducks in a row and

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:24.549
We did this because it's been shown in many animals that the animals at an edge of a group spend more time being vigilant

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:29.169
like antelope in Africa if they're at the edge of the group they lift their head up and

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:32.439
Interrupt their feeding more frequently than the ones in the center

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:39.520
Presumably the ones at the edge feel more exposed and more at risk of being eaten because the predator would get them first

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:41.980
so we put our ducks in a row and

00:14:42.589 --> 00:14:44.589
What we found was a very?

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:51.310
Dramatic effect of where they were in the group the birds that were in the more central

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:54.310
positions that were flanked by ducks on either side

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:59.950
They spent most of their time sleeping with both eyes closed and both halves of their brain

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:04.119
But when the birds were positioned at the end of the row they

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:12.429
Switched and they spent much more time sleeping with one eye open, and they showed a profound preference for directing that open

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:18.459
Eye away from the other birds as shown in the cartoon at the bottom, so this clearly showed that

00:15:19.279 --> 00:15:22.809
They have this capacity to sleep with one eye open

00:15:23.990 --> 00:15:28.810
And that it serves a function in allowing them to get some sleep without getting eaten

00:15:29.029 --> 00:15:35.649
And I believe we have an image of a seal as well if we could show that because this is so

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:43.060
This isn't just limited to birds. I mean so so there are other situations where animals will switch off

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:48.820
Yeah, it this seems to be a common trade among birds, but among mammals

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:56.619
This type of sleep is only found in marine mammals its found in and the cetaceans the dolphins and whales

00:15:57.140 --> 00:16:01.629
in some of the seals the first seal shown in this image and

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:03.500
also in

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:05.150
manatees and

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:11.919
In the case of this image. It's a first seal and what they do is the they have the awake?

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:15.870
Half of their brain in this case the right half

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:19.559
That's attached to the flipper down in the water and that flipper

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:26.399
paddles and keeps them in a position that allows them to keep their nose above the surface and that way they can sleep and

